C = [
("h1", "7. Dying with Him"),
("p", "Claim: in Romans 6, Galatians 2:20 and 6:14, and Colossians 2:20 and 3:3, Paul says that the believer died with Christ. The Cross is here not something done for the believer from outside, but something the believer is said to have shared. The problem the passages answer is sin’s mastery and the world’s claim. The result they state is freedom, a new walk and a life hid with Christ in God. The passages also say what the believer is to do with the fact, and the word they use is “reckon.”"),
("sub", "7.1 The question that opens Romans 6"),
("p", "“What shall we say then? Shall we continue in sin, that grace may abound? God forbid. How shall we, that are dead to sin, live any longer therein?” (Romans 6:1, 2). The question comes out of Romans 5, where grace is said to have abounded much more where sin abounded. Paul’s answer does not begin with a command. It begins with a fact about “we, that are dead to sin.” The Cross appears in the answer as the event in which that death took place."),
("sub", "7.2 “Baptized into his death” (Romans 6:3–5)"),
("p", "“Know ye not, that so many of us as were baptized into Jesus Christ were baptized into his death? Therefore we are buried with him by baptism into death: that like as Christ was raised up from the dead by the glory of the Father, even so we also should walk in newness of life. For if we have been planted together in the likeness of his death, we shall be also in the likeness of his resurrection” (Romans 6:3–5). The baptism of the passage is a baptism “into Jesus Christ” and “into his death.” The volume reads it as the baptism by the Spirit into the Body of Christ, the one baptism, and not as water baptism. The study on the one baptism sets out that reading from the letters, and this volume follows it."),
("p", "Thomas Constable, on the parallel in Colossians 2:12, writes that “Baptism here (v. 12) refers to Spirit baptism, which is symbolized by water baptism.” The volume agrees with the first half of that sentence. The second half, that water baptism symbolizes it, is a further claim that the passages in Romans 6 and Colossians 2 do not make in their words, and the volume does not make it."),
("sub", "7.3 “Crucified with him” and “reckon” (Romans 6:6–11)"),
("p", "“Knowing this, that our old man is crucified with him, that the body of sin might be destroyed, that henceforth we should not serve sin. For he that is dead is freed from sin” (Romans 6:6, 7). The verb is in the perfect: the old man is crucified with him. The purpose is stated: that the body of sin might be destroyed and that henceforth we should not serve sin. The statement is made as a thing known, “knowing this,” before any command is given."),
("p", "The command comes in verse 11: “Likewise reckon ye also yourselves to be dead indeed unto sin, but alive unto God through Jesus Christ our Lord.” The verb is “reckon,” and the thing reckoned is a fact already stated. The order of the passage is plain. First the fact: the old man is crucified with him. Then the response: reckon yourselves to be dead indeed unto sin. The response is not what makes the fact true, and the fact is not what makes the response unnecessary. The volume holds both in the order Paul gives them."),
("tbl", "Romans 6:3–11, the fact and the response.", [
    ("Verse", "KJV text", "What to observe"),
    ("Rom 6:3", "“baptized into Jesus Christ were baptized into his death”", "Into Christ, into his death"),
    ("Rom 6:4", "“buried with him by baptism into death”", "Buried with him; purpose: walk in newness of life"),
    ("Rom 6:6", "“our old man is crucified with him”", "Old man crucified; purpose: body of sin destroyed"),
    ("Rom 6:7", "“he that is dead is freed from sin”", "Freed from sin"),
    ("Rom 6:8", "“if we be dead with Christ, we believe that we shall also live with him”", "Dead with Christ; living with him is believed, in the future"),
    ("Rom 6:10", "“in that he died, he died unto sin once”", "Once; the death was to sin"),
    ("Rom 6:11", "“reckon ye also yourselves to be dead indeed unto sin”", "The response: reckon"),
  ], [0.14, 0.50, 0.36]),
("sub", "7.4 Colossians 2:20 and 3:3"),
("p", "“Wherefore if ye be dead with Christ from the rudiments of the world, why, as though living in the world, are ye subject to ordinances” (Colossians 2:20). The setting is a letter to people offered ordinances: “Touch not; taste not; handle not” (Colossians 2:21). Paul does not answer by listing the ordinances’ faults. He answers with the fact: ye be dead with Christ from the rudiments of the world. Then: “If ye then be risen with Christ, seek those things which are above, where Christ sitteth on the right hand of God. Set your affection on things above, not on things on the earth. For ye are dead, and your life is hid with Christ in God” (Colossians 3:1–3)."),
("p", "The death with Christ is the ground of the answer to the ordinances, and the risen life is the ground of the exhortation to seek things above. The passage does not describe how the believer died. It says that he did, and it draws a conclusion. The Cross here is something in which the believer’s own relation to the world has been settled."),
("sub", "7.5 Galatians 2:20 and 6:14 again"),
("p", "The same pattern is in Galatians. “I am crucified with Christ: nevertheless I live” (Galatians 2:20) states the fact and then states the life. “By whom the world is crucified unto me, and I unto the world” (Galatians 6:14) states the Cross as the point at which two things were separated: Paul and the world. The two statements are in different parts of the letter and answer different pressures. The first answers the claim that righteousness comes by the law. The second answers the claim that Paul’s opponents had to glory in the flesh."),
("sub", "7.6 What the passages leave unsaid"),
("bul", [
    "They do not say that the believer’s death with Christ is a work the believer performs. “Crucified with him” is stated as a fact, and “reckon” is the response.",
    "They do not say that the believer ceases to sin, or that the old man is gone from experience. Romans 6:12–14 gives commands, which would be needless if sin were no longer a pressure.",
    "They do not say that dying with Christ is the same as Christ’s dying for the sins of the believer. In Romans 6 the death is “unto sin”; in Romans 5 it is “for the ungodly.” The prepositions differ and the volume keeps them apart.",
    "They do not say that water baptism is the means. The volume reads the baptism as the Spirit’s, following the study on the one baptism.",
  ]),
]
